Now I have all the data needed. Here's the performance analysis:

---

## .NET Performance Analysis — `StringReadability.cs`

**Context:** Called on every user-facing string in a CMS rendering hundreds of dynamic strings per page — this is a **hot path**.

### Scan Execution Checklist

| Recipe | Hits |
|--------|------|
| `.ToLower()`/`.ToUpper()` | 2 |
| `params` array allocation | 1 |
| `.All()`/`.Any()` LINQ on chars | 2 |
| `.Select`/`.Cast`/`.Aggregate` | 5 |
| Unsealed classes | 3 |
| Sealed classes | 0 |
| `GeneratedRegex` (positive) | 2 |
| `RegexOptions.Compiled` (fallback) | 2 |

---

### 🟡 Findings (all Moderate — elevated to hot-path severity)

#### 1. LINQ chain in `FromPascalCase` allocates enumerator + delegate per call (1 instance)
**Impact:** `.Matches().Cast<Match>().Select(...)` allocates 3+ objects (enumerator, delegate, intermediate collections) on every humanization call. At hundreds of calls/page, this is significant GC pressure.
**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L86-98`
**Fix:** Replace with a `foreach` loop over `MatchCollection` (which is directly enumerable in .NET 7+) and build the result with a `ValueStringBuilder` or `Span<char>` buffer:
```csharp
internal static string FromPascalCase(string input)
{
    var matches = PascalCaseWordPartsRegex().Matches(input);
    if (matches.Count == 0) return input;

    var sb = new StringBuilder(input.Length + matches.Count);
    for (int i = 0; i < matches.Count; i++)
    {
        if (i > 0) sb.Append(' ');
        var value = matches[i].Value;
        bool allUpper = true;
        foreach (char c in value) { if (!char.IsUpper(c)) { allUpper = false; break; } }
        if (allUpper && (value.Length > 1 || (matches[i].Index > 0 && input[matches[i].Index - 1] == ' ') || value == "I"))
            sb.Append(value);
        else
            sb.Append(value.ToLowerInvariant());
    }

    string result = sb.ToString();
    // Check all-uppercase-with-spaces
    bool onlyUpperOrSpace = true;
    bool hasSpace = false;
    foreach (char c in result)
    {
        if (c == ' ') hasSpace = true;
        else if (!char.IsUpper(c)) { onlyUpperOrSpace = false; break; }
    }
    if (onlyUpperOrSpace && hasSpace)
        result = result.ToLowerInvariant();

    return result.Length > 0
        ? string.Concat(char.ToUpper(result[0]).ToString().AsSpan(), result.AsSpan(1))
        : result;
}
```

#### 2. `.All(char.IsUpper)` LINQ on string allocates enumerator (2 instances)
**Impact:** Iterating a `string` via LINQ allocates a `CharEnumerator` object per call. Called per-match inside the Select lambda, this multiplies with word count.
**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L93`, `StringReadability.cs:L101`
**Fix:** Replace with a `foreach` loop or manual char scan (shown in fix above).

#### 3. `.ToLower()` without culture — allocates + culture-dependent (2 instances)
**Impact:** Each `.ToLower()` allocates a new string using current culture rules. Use `.ToLowerInvariant()` for deterministic behavior and avoid the culture lookup overhead.
**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L97`, `StringReadability.cs:L103`
**Fix:** Replace `.ToLower()` with `.ToLowerInvariant()`.

#### 4. `params IStringTransformer[]` allocates array on every call (1 instance)
**Impact:** Every `.Transform(To.TitleCase)` call allocates a 1-element array. Called inside `Pascalize()` which is called per-word in `Dehumanize()`.
**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L213`
**Fix:** Add a 1-argument overload:
```csharp
public static string Transform(this string input, IStringTransformer transformer) =>
    transformer.Transform(input);
```

#### 5. `Dehumanize` calls `Humanize().Pascalize()` per word via LINQ (1 instance)
**Impact:** `words.Select(word => word.Humanize().Pascalize())` allocates delegate + enumerator, and each word goes through full regex-based humanization then title-casing + `.Replace(" ", "")` — extremely allocation-heavy for the compound chain.
**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L171`
**Fix:** Use a `StringBuilder` loop and avoid re-humanizing already-split words:
```csharp
var sb = new StringBuilder(input.Length);
foreach (var word in words)
{
    string pascalized = word.Humanize().Pascalize();
    sb.Append(pascalized);
}
return sb.ToString();
```

#### 6. `Pascalize()` calls `.Replace(" ", "")` — allocates even when no spaces (1 instance)
**Impact:** `.Humanize().Transform(To.TitleCase).Replace(" ", "")` is a 3-allocation chain (humanize result, title-case result, replace result) on every call.
**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L187`
**Fix:** Combine into a single pass that title-cases and removes spaces simultaneously.

#### 7. Three unsealed internal classes (3 instances)
**Impact:** `ToTitleCase`, `ToLowerCase`, `ToUpperCase` are never subclassed. Sealing enables JIT devirtualization of `IStringTransformer.Transform()` calls.
**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L226`, `L232`, `L237`
**Fix:** Add `sealed` keyword to each class declaration.

---

### ✅ Positive Findings

- ✅ `[GeneratedRegex]` used with `#if NET7_0_OR_GREATER` — best practice for source-gen regex
- ✅ `RegexOptions.Compiled` as fallback for older TFMs — correct pattern
- ✅ `Concat(char, ReadOnlySpan<char>)` uses `stackalloc` — zero heap allocation
- ✅ `string.Concat(ReadOnlySpan, ReadOnlySpan)` — efficient span concat

---

### Summary

| Severity | Count | Top Issue |
|----------|-------|-----------|
| 🔴 Critical | 0 | — |
| 🟡 Moderate | 7 | LINQ + delegate allocation in `FromPascalCase` per-call on hot path |
| ℹ️ Info | 0 | — |

**Estimated per-call allocation savings:** Fixing findings 1-6 would eliminate ~8-12 object allocations and ~4 intermediate string allocations per `Humanize()` invocation. At hundreds of calls per page render, this could meaningfully reduce Gen0 GC collections.

